WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.119
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:12.940
We still have institutions
that are too big to fail.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:18.350
And don't let anybody tell you otherwise,
because all the stuff they say

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:24.750
about how they can now fail without
causing collateral harm is not true.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:30.590
These are institutions that are bigger
than any institution by its size.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:36.920
Not Apple, not Facebook, not anybody
is as big as say JP Morgan Chase.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.424
They're all connected
together in a big knot,

00:00:40.424 --> 00:00:45.679
they're all together of a house of
cards that all works while it works,

00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:51.562
and when the next time something really
starts rattling it, is going to be scary.

00:00:51.562 --> 00:00:56.785
[MUSIC]

00:00:56.785 --> 00:01:04.670
Misconduct pays to the people across
the board, lower level, higher level.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:10.200
And as a society, we kind of become
a little bit helpless to prevent it,

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.800
all we can do is we can't send
a corporation to jail and

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.010
then we have not enough means to show
criminality of individuals because it's

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:24.060
all very diffused and very deniable and
difficult to prove in court or whatever.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.590
Or we don't spend the resources
on these kinds of white collar

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.910
prosecutions in a corporate context, who
knows all the combination of reasons but.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:38.848
Bottom line is the consequences for
individuals are usually minimal even when

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:42.078
a lot of wrongdoing is done
in a corporate context.

00:01:42.078 --> 00:01:47.523
[MUSIC]

00:01:47.523 --> 00:01:51.724
You have people who are angry
with a rigged system, and yet

00:01:51.724 --> 00:01:58.060
they don't completely understand the ways
in which the system is really rigged.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:03.410
And then they are prone to be in directed

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:08.080
to be angry at the wrong targets,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.630
at the media, at immigrants,
at certain countries.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:17.516
And without actually looking
at some of the issues

00:02:17.516 --> 00:02:22.402
that are really corroding and,
distorting, and

00:02:22.402 --> 00:02:27.074
corrupting, the democracy
that we have here.

00:02:27.074 --> 00:02:32.377
[MUSIC]

00:02:32.377 --> 00:02:38.068
I like to give examples
of safety of buildings or

00:02:38.068 --> 00:02:42.351
airplanes or bridges or whatever.

00:02:42.351 --> 00:02:47.356
Here, the public demand safety
doesn't need to know the details

00:02:47.356 --> 00:02:52.089
of the engineering to kind of
demand that that there is a system

00:02:52.089 --> 00:02:56.839
that creates safe buildings,
safe bridges, safe flights.

00:02:56.839 --> 00:03:00.523
And they will be immediate consequences
for everybody involved and

00:03:00.523 --> 00:03:04.750
we wouldn't even know who's responsible,
when that doesn't work.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.112
In the financial system it's
virtually the opposite,

00:03:08.112 --> 00:03:12.689
the recklessness is just spread out and
there's no direct line between what

00:03:12.689 --> 00:03:15.712
somebody does and
a little bit of excessive rest.

00:03:15.712 --> 00:03:18.880
Did they take or
the extra long they make or not make.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.260
And sort of the outcomes and
it's very easy for

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.350
the public to get completely
confused about what's going on.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.699
What the public needs is
to see the problem first,

00:03:31.699 --> 00:03:36.104
because recognizing the problem
is the first step to solving it.

00:03:36.104 --> 00:03:44.291
[MUSIC]

